\tag{connes}
\tag{lean}
\tag{operator-algebra}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Why formalize the counterexamples?}

\p{\strong{Mathematical motivation.} The counterexample is not merely a
negative answer to Connes' conjecture. Its richer content is a controlled
experiment in nonfaithfulness. The three proof families vary different
algebraic inputs while preserving the analytic output: OpenAI varies the
abelian kernel, Zhou fixes the kernel and varies the action and module
structure, and Anthropic fixes both and varies the extension class
\citet{Chapter 4}{openai2026tenadvances}
\citet{Sections 1 and 3}{zhou2026icc}
\citet{Sections 3--5 and Remark 7.1}{anthropic2026icc}. In every case the group
distinction survives algebraically but disappears after measurable
crossed-product transport. Particularly striking are an algebraically
nontrivial cocycle becoming measurably removable, distinct module actions
becoming conjugate after Fourier--Pontryagin transport, and
characteristic-two extension data distinguishing groups while their factors
remain isomorphic. Property (T) acts as a control condition: these are rigid
groups, not examples explained by group-theoretic flexibility. The organizing
question is which algebraic distinctions survive representation, twisting,
duality, and analytic completion. A cautious physical analogy is that distinct
microscopic algebraic models may determine the same observable operator
algebra; no physical model is asserted here.}

\p{\strong{Formalization motivation.} The formalization asks how much of that
information-loss taxonomy can surface in explicit proof interfaces. In this
development, the square-span certificate,
Fourier shear, measurable transport, continuous-coefficient closure, spectral
criterion, characteristic-kernel argument, and quotient-twisted module
obstruction expose where the proof needs more than routine translation. The
result is an auditable dependency map for the claim that the factor forgets
structure. As a demanding consumer of Mathlib and TauCeti, it both reuses
existing algebraic, analytic, and representation-theoretic infrastructure and
identifies contributions that would make later formalizations less bespoke. The concrete
proof design is summarized in \ref{connes-000K}; the present library boundary
is recorded in \ref{connes-000G}.}

\p{\strong{Agent use and human digestion.} This project also tests a workflow
for preparing a draft formalization for human examination: ground
claims in the papers, keep a declaration-level provenance ledger, accompany
the code with mathematical and design notes, separate technical proof replay
from mathematical-fidelity review, record unresolved boundaries, and retain
human control over corrections and landing. The public
\href{https://github.com/utensil/connes-rigidity/blob/main/docs/AUDITS.md}{audit ledger},
\href{https://github.com/utensil/connes-rigidity/blob/main/docs/PROVENANCE.md}{provenance ledger},
and
\href{https://github.com/utensil/connes-rigidity/blob/main/docs/COMPARATOR.md}{Comparator trust-boundary note}
make those roles explicit. This is a draft prepared for human examination, not
a claim of independent peer review.}
